SM(219,{"n":"Genesis 1.1-8","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Genesis 1.1-8.mp3","d":2772.4,"s":[[0.24,"Come and study the Holy Scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.96,"Father Stephen holds a PhD in Biblical Studies, and is an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[20.64,"We'll go ahead and get started."],[21.92,"When we get started in just a moment, we'll be starting in Genesis 1, verse 1."],[28.64,"So that'll be relatively easy to find."],[35.36,"So I'm not gonna do uh a long review of the introduction that we did last time."],[44.8,"Um so I will just say that uh by the time people are hearing this recording, at least, the introduction will already be there for people to go back and listen to."],[54.72,"Um I'm gonna add a couple of things real quick that I realized I didn't. mention in the introduction"],[63.96,"The main one being I didn't talk about the names of the books in the Torah or the Pentateuch."],[72.36,"Because the names we know them by the Greek names for the books."],[83.0,"So Genesis, Yenesis, is basically beginning."],[93.77,"It's the coming into being, the beginning."],[95.87,"The generation comes from the same."],[100.75,"The um and then Exodus literally means the way or the road out in Greek."],[108.95,"Uh Leviticus, you know, Levitikos uh or Luitikos uh means uh the Levite thing, literally."],[121.67,"The Levitical book is the thing in this case."],[125.51,"Umbers is kind of obvious."],[131.8,"That's not actually the Greek name."],[134.36,"That's the English translation of the Greek name through Latin."],[138.76,"And then Deuteronomy is Devteros Nomos, which is literally like the second law or the second T.orah"],[150.0,"Because most of the stuff that's in Deuteronomy is stuff that was already in the previous four books."],[157.44,"So for example, in Deuteronomy 5, we get the Ten Commandments again that were originally in Exodus chapter 20."],[166.0,"So those are the names we know them by."],[170.64,"The Hebrew names, um, this is gonna be a good segue here in Genesis."],[176.72,"The Hebrew names for the books are the first word."],[182.16,"Just whatever the first word is."],[184.8,"So the Hebrew name of Genesis is Bereshit, which uh is what we translate in the beginning."],[192.96,"Um which is a little bit interpretive"],[197.72,"So technically, Bereshit means more like at the head."],[203.8,"Because uh in Hebrew."],[209.72,"The b is a uh preposition that can mean in, at, or with, which makes uh Hebrew really fun."],[221.48,"In fact, the prepositions can mean like a whole bunch of different things."],[224.84,"Yes, ma'am. So um uh I guess it was a couple of weeks ago."],[229.04,"Um I was listening to a video and the person show that it means home."],[234.12,"Is there a connection that one?"],[236.16,"Or that's just sort of a final variation."],[240.64,"I don't know where they're getting that."],[242.72,"Okay. Yeah, because it literally means head."],[247.92,"But head can mean, for example, the head of a river."],[251.86,"It's like the source of the river."],[254.42,"It's where the river begins."],[259.7,"Uh the other another place where we know that word from is Rosh Hashanah."],[263.94,"Rosh Hashanah, which literally means the head of the year."],[270.12,"And that's New Year's."],[271.16,"It's the first day."],[273.34,"So you could say the beginning of the year."],[277.18,"Um so, but yeah, so that's the name of Genesis, and likewise, and we'll mention this again as we eventually get to the other books in the Torah or the Pentateuch, um, what the first word is."],[291.14,"But it's not they did not make an attempt to sort of characterize what the book was about, or I mean, Exodus, for example, is very clearly saying, well, this is what the book is about."],[303.83,"Um, whereas just the first word is just this is the first word."],[309.75,"Uh and that's how it's known."],[313.27,"Um so that covered."],[316.71,"We'll now get into the first word."],[320.95,"Um, unless there are any questions or comments or accusations. of heresy leveled at me that anybody wants to make"],[336.75,"So Genesis 1 1 is in the beginning, God made heaven and earth."],[344.59,"That's how it is in the Orthodox Study Bible, at least."],[348.03,"So that seems simple and straightforward, but there's there's sort of more to this."],[357.31,"So the the um the Hebrew of that is uh Bereishit Bara Elohim Hashamayam Vaharas, which is that Bereshit word, which we'll come back to, that we translate in the beginning, that first word."],[376.8,"Bera is the word for create."],[381.6,"So we'll come back to that too."],[385.86,"Elohim is God."],[389.3,"Uh Hashemayim is uh the word for the heavens."],[395.78,"Mayam, which you can hear in there, is the word for uh waters."],[403.86,"And we'll see when we get to the second day of creation, how those are connected here."],[410.22,"And Aritz is the earth, the ground."],[416.91,"Um so uh we'll start with that first word."],[423.39,"Um we because we translate it in the beginning, we're used to thinking of this as being a reference to time."],[439.73,"Sort of this is the first thing that happened."],[445.14,"But neither Bareshi, nor the Greek translation of that, which is an Archi."],[453.66,"right Right? And being in Archi being literally first or primary."],[459.14,"It's a really direct translation of head"],[463.42,"Um, like an archon."],[466.08,"What occupies the first place."],[468.4,"And it really means more like in the first place."],[475.2,"Or to begin, in terms of what's being communicated to us."],[483.9,"Um, and this is important because this first sentence, this first verse, is kind of a summary statement."],[496.28,"So it's not that God created the heavens and the earth, and then this other stuff that we're about to read about happens after that."],[506.21,"It's this is a statement of what's about to be narrated."],[512.53,"So in the first place, or to begin, God created the heavens and the earth, and now we're gonna hear a description of that."],[520.69,"Now that's gonna be described to us in a particular way that St."],[535.25,"John's Gospel begins."],[538.21,"Enarchie. In the beginning was the word."],[542.21,"So St John uses Genesis a lot all the way through his gospel, but even right there at the beginning, he's trying to line up right what he's doing with what Genesis is doing."],[556.97,"And in the same way, we call those first 18 verses of St."],[560.57,"John's Gospel his prologue, where he's making this summary statement, and then he goes and describes the life of Christ."],[576.01,"So then the other thing I said we were going to come back to was what's translated here as made."],[582.65,"Which is probably better translated created"],[587.73,"Because in both Hebrew and Greek, the word for create and the word for make are different words."],[600.62,"So in Hebrew, bara is to create."],[604.94,"Asa is to do or to make."],[608.46,"In Greek, ktizo is to create."],[613.5,"Pieo is to do or to make."],[617.74,"Those are two different words."],[621.98,"And to the idea of creating means what it originally meant in English, so you're making something that didn't exist before."],[634.48,"That something is coming to be that before that wasn't."],[638.64,"Whereas making, does not imply that."],[643.06,"Verb to do or to make, you might be doing something you've done a thousand times before."],[648.12,"You might be making your 853rd share."],[652.85,"Um, you're just making something."],[656.15,"Out of other things."],[658.09,"Using raw materials and that kind of thing."],[660.73,"Um that is not to say, here's where the accusations of heresy come in."],[665.85,"Um, the Orthodox Church teaches, and the scriptures teach that God created everything from nothing."],[675.53,"Fancy Latin way, creation, ex nihilo"],[680.87,"But Genesis 1, as we're about to see, does not describe this particular passage, does not describe God creating from nothing in the way that we typically think about that, as modern people."],[697.27,"There are places later in the Old Testament that do that."],[701.05,"And so that's true."],[702.89,"But this place comes at it from a different perspective."],[707.85,"The story of God creating the world is actually told several times in the Old Testament."],[713.21,"In different ways."],[715.22,"They all agree God created the world, but they're making different points."],[718.5,"Just like we have four Gospels"],[721.68,"They aren't all identical."],[724.32,"But they're all describing the same Christ."],[726.98,"Who actually lived and did these things."],[729.78,"But to make different points and from different perspectives."],[733.86,"But so the idea here is that God is making the world in that, or creating the world in that there wasn't a world before this."],[748.3,"Because for most ancient people time was basically seen as cyclical."],[759.42,"And so you could have an endless number of worlds before this one."],[767.02,"In fact, pretty much everybody believed that there had been at least one before this one."],[772.06,"Usually ended with a flood."],[775.82,"Pretty much across the board."],[777.1,"But some places you find where there's lots of lots."],[781.56,"This is there is no world."],[786.65,"There are no heavens, there is no earth, and God creates them."],[792.25,"This also says something about God."],[796.73,"Because the gods of ancient peoples, there's pretty much universally found outside of Israel, some versionion of the success myth."],[806.73,"That's what scholars call it"],[810.93,"That at some point in the past there were different gods who ruled over the world."],[818.21,"And then there was some kind of insurrection or revolution."],[821.97,"Usually it's some son of those older gods goes and defeats and or kills and or maims their father and takes over."],[835.89,"And those stories, sometimes you get multiple levels of succession."],[844.85,"So probably the one that most of us are most familiar with is like Greek mythology."],[849.97,"Greek mythology, you've got uh Uranos, the heavens, and and Gaia, the earth."],[858.04,"And then they have children who are the Titans, and Kronos maims his father, Uranos, and takes over, and then Zeus, who's Kronos' son, leads his his uh siblings to go and defeat the Titans, and they take over."],[877.66,"There's like two cycles."],[880.22,"Uh Babylon, there were several."],[883.74,"Um, and so God sort of addresses this directly."],[888.46,"For example, in Isaiah, he says, Yahweh, the God of Israel, says, I am God, before me there was no no other, and after me there will be other."],[899.16,"That he's God all the way."],[901.0,"But that's already embedded here."],[904.86,"At the beginning, because there is no world before God makes one, which means there can't have been any gods before him, there can't have been any anything before him."],[917.35,"That's already that's already put in place here."],[922.23,"And this also means that even though God is going to come to identify himself as the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, the God of Israel, the God of these particular people, he's not just the God of some place or something."],[938.09,"He's not a storm God"],[939.75,"He's not the God of war."],[942.95,"He's not, because none of those things exist until he creates them."],[949.61,"So he's not associated with any of those things."],[952.57,"He makes all of them."],[953.77,"They don't pre exist him or coexist with him."],[962.81,"So there's a lot in that first verse."],[967.93,"So verse 2 the earth was invisible and unfinished, and darkness was over the deep."],[977.61,"The Spirit of God was hovering over the face of the water."],[984.85,"So I'm going to do a little more Hebrew on the first part of this verse just because it's fun to say."],[989.73,"The Hebrew is that the earth was Tohuva Bohu."],[995.01,"Which is just fun in rhymes."],[1003.57,"And that's what's being translated here as invisible and unfinished."],[1010.45,"But in the old King James, they translate it as without form and void."],[1017.81,"And that's tohu, kind of means that."],[1019.81,"It means like formless or chaotic, disordered."],[1024.95,"And bohu. is the void part, meaning empty"],[1033.5,"So this is where we get into how this is a little different than how we think about nothing."],[1042.7,"So when I say the word nothing to you, I'm guessing here, but I imagine the image, if you try to imagine nothing, would probably be like a black space or just a white space."],[1060.43,"But that's not actually nothing."],[1062.19,"That's an empty space."],[1064.77,"And an empty space is sort of something."],[1067.09,"We can't imagine nothing."],[1067.73,"imagine nothing Anytime we try tohing or picture it, that picture is somet."],[1074.4,"So um nothing is something we can't conceive."],[1079.52,"Um ancient people didn't even try to make that move."],[1087.22,"Of trying to imagine nothingness."],[1089.14,"For ancient people, being wasn't opposed to nothingness."],[1095.78,"Apologies to Jean-Paul Sartre."],[1097.83,"But being was opposed to chaos."],[1103.11,"To exist meant to be not just to exist in general, but to be something in particular."],[1107.99,"something in particular Everything that exists is."],[1115.94,"Um, you don't have something that just exists."],[1120.62,"That's but isn't anything."],[1123.18,"Um Are you coming around to saying that matter is eternal?"],[1126.94,"No. No, because what we think of matter is not what they thought of."],[1135.59,"So when we think of matter, we just think of the smallest particles of stuff that we can imagine."],[1144.6,"You know. Matter is just stuff, physical stuff."],[1148.62,"That you can touch."],[1150.12,"Um, yeah, but even then photon, however, there's some right, um, little bundles of energy."],[1158.28,"Um, and what they meant when they said matter, like when Aristotle talks about prime matter, is again chaos."],[1169.64,"Prime matter it isn't anything, but it has the potential to become anything."],[1179.4,"Well, except those are stem cells."],[1182.2,"And this is nothing."],[1184.97,"This is also a thing we can't imagine."],[1187.9,"But the difference is that the core difference is that this we're not talking about a void."],[1196.1,"So is it kind of like the scientific relationship between matter and energy?"],[1202.1,"Like energy. Not really."],[1204.26,"Okay. Both energy and matter are something."],[1207.06,"Okay. So this is this is sort of, and again, it's something we can't really imagine."],[1214.95,"But it's sort of, there is all of this potentiality there that's unactualized."],[1223.43,"So it isn't anything."],[1226.44,"And what might help a little with this."],[1228.62,"Because we're being given imagery, the imagery that then gets used in the rest of the verse."],[1236.86,"It says the Spirit of God was hovering over the face of the water"],[1243.04,"So water again in the Old Testament, water, we've talked about this several times."],[1247.12,"Water represents chaos."],[1248.64,"This is another image for chaos."],[1251.33,"But the word that's translated hover here, and uh in the Hebrew, and St."],[1260.05,"Basil actually points this out."],[1261.89,"He didn't know Hebrew, but he points to the Syriac word that was used here."],[1267.98,"Which is Syriac is a dialect of Aramaic."],[1271.5,"Is a word that's used to describe a bird, a mother bird brooding over its eggs."],[1285.22,"So that egg imagery is another way at getting at this idea of potential."],[1292.23,"So it's something more like possibility."],[1294.55,"Yeah. Because it's not manifest or something."],[1298.86,"And so what God's gonna now do as we go forward in the rest of this chapter is he's gonna take that."],[1306.32,"The eggs are gonna hatch, as it were."],[1308.61,"And this is going to be put in order."],[1314.26,"And established. So that's why I say this particular passage is not aimed at. creation ex nihilo the way we usually think about it"],[1326.28,"There are other passages in the Old Testament that directly teach that."],[1330.6,"But this passage is aimed at describing God as taking these things out of the realm of potential and bringing them into the realm of being, of existence."],[1345.0,"Is there room for anticipation of that's part?"],[1350.84,"Well, that's the brooding."],[1352.9,"That's the spirit there."],[1355.28,"Um, yeah. Yeah."],[1358.08,"So this isn't just hovering as in like but um. but actively right brooding"],[1369.4,"And that imagery then gets picked up at Christ's baptism when the Holy Spirit descends like a dove."],[1375.73,"This is where that bird imagery."],[1378.05,"Christ is in the water."],[1381.31,"And and in the hymns, it's a few weeks away still, but in the hymns, it keeps uh connecting Christ to Adam and redeeming Adam in the waters."],[1396.83,"And that's through this imagery."],[1401.2,"So the the Tohu Vabohu here."],[1403.78,"The without form and void, these are presented as these two problems that God's going to solve."],[1408.58,"The first one of those problems, the Tohu, is formless"],[1415.9,"It's chaos. It needs to be put in order."],[1420.27,"Brought out of chaos into existence."],[1423.79,"And to exist means to be in an order."],[1426.83,"Not just the thing itself is in a certain order and is what it is, but there's an order between things."],[1435.88,"There's this whole system of relationships, correct relationships between things."],[1442.36,"And that system of relationships is what's going to be called justice later on in the Old Testament."],[1449.8,"Justice and then peace are that state. of everything being in the right place again"],[1455.92,"But there's also going to be injustice, as we're going to see, where things get out of order."],[1459.7,"Where that order gets broken."],[1461.94,"But so when God creates, he's going to be taking out of that chaos and putting things into order, internal and with each other."],[1470.84,"In the creation."],[1472.44,"That's problem one."],[1474.2,"Problem two, the bohu is that it's empty."],[1477.48,"Empty specifically of life."],[1481.88,"Because chaos and life are opposed to each other."],[1487.04,"This is one of the reasons why the ocean is uh an image the sea is an image of chaos and death because humans can't live there."],[1496.28,"In fact, they drown"],[1502.54,"Um it's threatening."],[1507.15,"So those are the two things, being empty of life and being chaotic chaotic and disordered."],[1513.39,"Those are the two problems with creation that God is going to resolve through creation as it goes forward."],[1522.36,"And as you mentioned, there's this sense of anticipation already with the the spirit of God there."],[1535.4,"Verse three. Then God said, Let there be light, and there was light."],[1539.4,"light. God saw the, it was good"],[1545.84,"And God divided the light from the darkness."],[1549.36,"God called the light day, the darkness he called night."],[1553.36,"And there was evening and morning one day."],[1557.92,"In the Hebrew, it's actually the opposite."],[1559.6,"It's day one."],[1563.68,"At the end."],[1565.06,"So God says, let there be light."],[1568.9,"Light comes to be."],[1570.68,"And then he separates light and darkness."],[1574.28,"So this is step one of that ordering, of dealing with that first problem of putting things in order."],[1580.68,"But apparently, light is not yet the sun, because the sun is going to be made light right but we have light and darkness separated right separated out from each other and so this is the first part of ordering right the first part of ordering."],[1597.76,"So that when light was created it was created and still somehow makes with darkness and needs to be separated."],[1606.32,"Yes. Yes. So there are two aspects of the say that well no, because he he God doesn't create darkness."],[1617.36,"Remember, darkness was over the face of the deep, which is the abyss in Greek, uh so God doesn't God isn't described here as creating the darkness."],[1628.1,"creates He the light."],[1630.86,"St. John's going to play with this in his prologue."],[1632.86,"Remember the light shown in the darkness, and the darkness didn't."],[1637.47,"So in the icon of the first day of creation, the light that is being created, has all of these angelic faces and wings in it."],[1658.15,"So the way that a lot of fathers interpret this, and I say a lot of fathers because there is some disagreement on this, but a lot of fathers interpret this as actually the creation of the angels, of the angelic beings."],[1675.15,"And then they connect the separation of the light from the darkness as referring to angels fallen and unfallen."],[1685.95,"That's not all of the fathers."],[1689.79,"A lot of other fathers connected to the fourth day, and we'll talk about that more when we get there, which is the sun and the moon and the second day um for different reasons right um but so the light here that we're talking about, because we're not talking about the sun and moon, this is this is heavenly light so you either get this is sort of the light of the glory of God himself shining in creation and driving out darkness or if this is light in the sense of the angelic beings who sort of have reflected or refracted."],[1738.81,"Darkness is not abolished light of the glory of God."],[1741.66,"Right it still exists or it I mean darkness is sort of the absence of light but yeah, yeah."],[1749.94,"Is this similar to the light that we that was talked about in revelation that came from the new Jerusalem?"],[1756.66,"Yes. Yeah. Yeah."],[1759.22,"This is what the fathers call the uh the uncreated light of God."],[1764.74,"If we're talking about God's light, if that's what's in view here, then this is the light of the glory of God Himself, and the difference is now that's shining into creation."],[1777.93,"That God is manifesting himself into creation."],[1781.61,"That is beginning to drive out darkness on that reading."],[1795.45,"So verse 6."],[1798.25,"Then God said, Let there be a firmament in the midst of the water, and let it divide the water from the water."],[1806.13,"And it was so."],[1807.73,"Thus God made the firmament, and God divided the water under the firmament from the water above the firmament."],[1814.29,"So God called the firmament heaven, and God saw that it was good."],[1818.45,"And there was evening and morning the second day."],[1822.53,"So even though the Greek word is pretty different than the Hebrew word here, they went with firmament in the Orthodox Study Bible, just because the King James Version sort of established that word as the word to use here."],[1835.77,"Um I've never seen that very used in any other place."],[1840.97,"It's kind of a punt."],[1842.25,"It's a little bit of a translation punt."],[1845.05,"So the the uh Hebrew word here is rakia, uh which means sort of like a barrier or a partition."],[1856.17,"Right would be how we understand it."],[1858.17,"And the visual image here is that this rakia, this firmament is sort of like a dome."],[1868.41,"And so there's water up above the dome, and there's water below the dome."],[1872.25,"So this is creating the sky."],[1875.22,"This is the heavens as in the sky."],[1878.28,"And then the sea are being separated."],[1883.08,"Specifically the sky, or like the sky is the separation with the water."],[1889.64,"The sky is the separation."],[1891.48,"Okay. Sky is the separation, and there there's water above."],[1894.12,"'s That's why it blue."],[1896.64,"Yeah, okay. Yes, because they're gonna say that's where storms come from."],[1908.22,"So they're gonna say you have storms up there and you have storms the sea."],[1913.36,"And then sort of in their cosmological understanding, God's throne, the angels and everything are up above that."],[1924.86,"They're up above that."],[1926.3,"And that's where we get the idea of like third heaven that you see later."],[1930.94,"Right. And there are various ways."],[1932.2,"When you when you get to Second Temple Judaism, there are all these different ways of categorizing the heavens and descriptions."],[1939.62,"There's sometimes seven, sometimes three, um, sometimes nine or ten."],[1946.74,"Um but those are all sort of up above this, is the idea."],[1951.48,"And um sometime early next year when we get to the flood, we'll see that the part of the way the flood is depicted is that the Rakhia, this firmament kind of opens up."],[1965.98,"To let the water dump back down."],[1968.51,"And that's that's part of what causes the uh the flood."],[1971.31,"But we'll talk about that more, that more then."],[1975.07,"Um I'll go ahead and say it here um to cover sort of the last two things."],[1988.71,"So we we we have this idea by putting things in order, God has also sort of created these spaces."],[1995.61,"In the sense that we now have day and night, we now have the sky and the sea."],[2001.55,"So things are being put in order, but they're still empty."],[2005.25,"There are now spaces, but they're empty spaces."],[2008.42,"But also, so as much as I hate going over this, um the question does arise how how literally are we expected to take this?"],[2021.39,"And uh we're we're not expected to believe the earth is flat."],[2026.99,"We're not expected to believe there's a literal dome of the sky."],[2031.65,"And that satellites are a trick."],[2034.23,"And that man never went to the moon."],[2035.93,"We're not expect right."],[2037.77,"Um as we talked about briefly last time."],[2042.3,"God is is not trying to sort of download some kind of future physics into Moses' brain."],[2052.42,"What we're reading here, according to the tradition of the church, is the vision that Moses received on Mount Sinai, the vision of the creation of the world."],[2062.74,"And so just like when we were reading Revelation and we were reading St."],[2066.22,"John's vision. And it was a mistake to say, like, oh, giant locusts that spit fire, those must be Apache attack helicopters."],[2075.49,"It's a mistake to try to literalize this into I'm a biblical flat earther."],[2082.99,"And the earth is flat and supported by pillars and all that."],[2090.11,"Yeah. So that's not what it's trying to convey."],[2094.03,"It's not trying to convey that kind of scientific information."],[2096.85,"This is a vision that Moses is receiving in order to communicate certain theological truths to him and truths about the world."],[2111.21,"Oh, go ahead."],[2113.93,"Yes. Well, see, that's that's an interesting question too."],[2120.3,"Because that's the next piece of this is how literally to take the time."],[2123.74,"Everybody will know that you're thinking about one day equals."],[2126.76,"Yeah, yeah. Time in general."],[2129.66,"So Yeah, and th this is this is an interesting question."],[2136.14,"So I mean that the the day question is implied by that."],[2142.16,"So because this is a vision that the prophet Moses is receiving."],[2150.58,"We wouldn't want to take the time so literally as to think that Moses sat there for 24 hours watching the things that we're told happened on the first day."],[2163.98,"Um, so that would be pushing it too far."],[2167.74,"Now when we get to the fourth day, and we see the sun and moon and stars are created, one of their purposes we're told in the text is going to be to measure times and seasons."],[2188.99,"Which, again, if we try to take this super literally causes an issue."],[2191.17,"y A da is a measure of time."],[2195.21,"Because you're going, well wait a minute, we just had three days."],[2200.55,"But we couldn't measure time until the fourth day."],[2203.93,"And so you can tie yourself up in a knot with that."],[2207.9,"So um I don't want I don't know how far to go down this rabbit hole."],[2216.62,"I never thought Jewish so the Jewish calendar begins on this day one or sometime in seven days."],[2226.54,"Do you think what is five thousand something right now?"],[2231.1,"I think so, yeah."],[2232.38,"Yeah. the Jewish calendar, yeah. Uh rabbinic"],[2235.14,"Yeah. So they count this, they have this, and and uh Matthew Henry did the same thing."],[2242.98,"Our Puritan friend Matthew Henry."],[2245.22,"Uh if you read his commentary on Genesis, he gives dates."],[2249.54,"He's like, this is April 24th, you know, four four thousand sixty-two BC."],[2256.74,"Like he's got it nailed right down."],[2259.14,"Um, yeah, so I think that's again an over an overline."],[2265.14,"I mean, uh what I'm saying, I don't know how far to go down this rabbit hole."],[2268.74,"What I mean is, um, since there aren't, I mean, Moses is seeing this vision, but when this is actually happening, long before Moses."],[2279.62,"There aren't any humans there to experience time"],[2286.6,"Well, that was a part of time."],[2288.44,"So, yeah. So then what do we even mean?"],[2294.12,"Because God doesn't experience time the way we do."],[2296.68,"And is time a consequence of the fall."],[2300.84,"No. Okay. No."],[2304.04,"But time the way we talk about it, the thing that you and I mean when we say time is a product of our human consciousness."],[2315.32,"Meaning, God doesn't experience time the way we do."],[2318.44,"That's reiterated to us in the Bible repeatedly."],[2321.98,"A thousand years are like a day, a day is like a thousand years."],[2324.44,"To try to communicate, again, that's not literal."],[2328.17,"But that the idea that God doesn't experience time the way we do."],[2334.18,"Which we would expect."],[2335.3,"And angels. If we say the angels are created on the first day, there's no reason to think they experience time the way we do."],[2343.34,"So if by time you mean a succession of events, well, yeah."],[2351.16,"Things happen and then other things happen."],[2354.48,"Um but what we usually call time is really our experience of time."],[2361.76,"Um so um yeah, and I don't know how far. we want to go down this philosophical rabbit hole"],[2368.08,"But there's a concept, there's a concept called deep time, which is hypothetical time that passed when there were no observers."],[2380.8,"Like there's there's no people that there's no one right observing anything."],[2392.14,"Yeah. Yeah, that it's hyper like we we talk about deep space, like beyond what we can observe."],[2398.46,"It's there, but we can't say anything about it, really."],[2402.7,"But it all sounds like like the possibility that we were talking about earlier."],[2408.06,"Like the possibility is there. but it hasn't it hasn't been formed yet"],[2414.1,"So it's like the possibility for time is there, night day, night possibility for light is there, and then we get the sun and then we get the moon."],[2425.46,"Yeah, put in order."],[2426.66,"Yeah. Yeah. So like just because it's not ordered, you doesn't mean the possibility that those things are."],[2433.03,"But a lot of times when we talk about a lot of times."],[2437.27,"Most often when we talk about time, we're talking about an order of it's precisely an order."],[2446.45,"So if you don't have that order yet, can you talk about time?"],[2453.11,"Yeah. It exploded people's brains"],[2455.15,"So I won't go any further down that rabbit hole."],[2460.59,"But but the idea is that that's enough that we don't want to try to force things into a very super mega literal mode."],[2473.07,"Um I thought we've seen practically everything."],[2479.07,"Has anybody else here ever seen the old 30s movie Green Pastors?"],[2483.07,"I have. Yeah."],[2487.95,"It's wonderful. It's based on a book that tells survival stories uh in a Negro dialect that now again you get called the racists we're doing."],[2495.55,"But yeah, and it that starts with this wonderful scene there in the fish fry head and then the little black angel set that says, Lord, the uh batteries support firmament."],[2498.59,"And the Lord says, That'd be a whole lot of firmament"],[2506.39,"And we end up with so much that the world is created."],[2508.63,"So mentioned that."],[2510.15,"Yeah, yeah. And this is also I mean, i there's a whole question too."],[2515.29,"Time doesn't come into existence because of the fall, but there is an open question, and this is in the fathers, as to whether time was experienced differently before the expulsion from paradise."],[2529.21,"And that's an important question because that gets into in the age to in the world to come, will we experience time differently?"],[2538.17,"We kind of assume because we don't know anything else, that time then will be like time now, and I'm using then and now as but right, that there'll be just sort of this succession of moments, but it's eternal life, so it'll just go on forever."],[2553.65,"It'll be forever young."],[2556.29,"Is it just a physical truth?"],[2559.89,"Physics truth that the younger you are, you experience time less speed."],[2569.33,"Well, I think that my theory is that that's based on the percentage of your life."],[2574.53,"Like when you're five, a year is 20% of your life."],[2578.37,"When you're 50, a year is 2% of your life."],[2581.31,"And so it seems, you know."],[2583.47,"Um my age years and techniques."],[2588.23,"So right, but so yeah, and so that that is a germane question in terms of time being experienced."],[2594.39,"But the important thing I think is that time is not this absolute thing that's out there in the world."],[2601.27,"Time is part of our experience of the world."],[2605.27,"The way we measure and experience time."],[2609.43,"Just like we don't see atoms."],[2612.31,"We don't experience them."],[2614.07,"Yeah. Yeah. That was our theory that if you could go into outer space and come back you'd be so much older or younger."],[2622.23,"Depending on how fast you travel."],[2624.07,"Yeah. Yeah. But yeah."],[2626.23,"And so like I know there's a everyday example."],[2629.31,"My dogs experience time very differently than me."],[2633.16,"Like I can go out to get the newspaper and come back in, or like Trisha and I could be go and be gone most of the day and come back, and they react exactly the same way."],[2646.44,"And I'm convinced that they don't really understand."],[2650.84,"Was this an hour?"],[2651.88,"Was this five hours?"],[2653.0,"Was this five minutes?"],[2655.0,"It's just they're gone."],[2656.2,"They're back. Yeah."],[2658.6,"They're gone. I'm sad."],[2659.72,"They're back. I'm happy."],[2660.84,"You know. So, um, and also kind of your explanation for visions of saints, saints having visions of saints who are not very much younger than when they died."],[2677.24,"Yeah But some of them are elderly like when they died, and why are some people in the afterlife appearing one way or the other?"],[2693.84,"And maybe it's just our trying to overlay our our understanding of time on top of whatever it really is."],[2703.52,"Yeah, we're filtering our perceptions through our understanding."],[2707.76,"Yeah, yeah. Yeah."],[2711.44,"Um, but yeah, one place that comes up in the in the fathers, and we'll get to that when we get into Genesis 3, is that a lot of the fathers don't have a big time lapse between the creation of humanity and the expulsion from paradise."],[2722.24,"Like it's almost immediate"],[2728.36,"And that would be the same idea for the fathers who understand that the angels are created and some of them fall on the first day."],[2738.7,"That that's because angels wouldn't experience time the way we do."],[2745.15,"So it's kind of immediate from their perspective."],[2750.03,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God."],[2757.39,"Father Stephen's email address is whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2762.75,"That's whole counsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":20.64,"lv":2,"h":"Names of the books (Greek)","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starting <b>Genesis 1:1</b> - no long review of the intro; it's posted to go back and hear</li><li class=\"d0\">Something he forgot: the <b>names</b> of the Torah books - we know them by <b>Greek names</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Genesis</b> = <i>genesis</i>: coming into being, <b>beginning</b>; \"generation\" same root</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Exodus</b> = \"the way or road <b>out</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Leviticus</b> = <i>Levitikos</i>, \"the <b>Levite</b> thing\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Numbers</b> - obvious; English translation of the Greek via <b>Latin</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Deuteronomy</b> = <i>Deuteros Nomos</i>, \"<b>second law</b>\" / second Torah</li><li class=\"d1\">most of it already in the previous four books</li><li class=\"d1\">e.g. <b>Deut 5</b>: Ten Commandments again, originally <b>Exodus 20</b></li></ul>"},{"t":170.64,"lv":2,"h":"Hebrew names: the first word","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew names = just the <b>first word</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Genesis = <i>Bereshit</i>, \"in the beginning\" - \"a little bit interpretive\"</li><li class=\"d1\">literally \"<b>at the head</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>b-</i> = preposition: <b>in, at, or with</b> - \"makes Hebrew really fun\"</li><li class=\"d1\">prepositions mean \"a whole bunch of different things\"</li><li class=\"d0\">No attempt to characterize the book's subject</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Exodus</b> clearly says what it's about; Hebrew name is just the <b>first word</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Will mention for the other books as we get to them</li></ul>"},{"t":224.84,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: head, source, Rosh Hashanah","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: video said it means \"<b>home</b>\" - a connection?</li><li class=\"d1\">doesn't know where they get that</li><li class=\"d0\">Literally <b>head</b></li><li class=\"d1\">head of a <b>river</b> = the <b>source</b>, where it begins</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Rosh Hashanah</b> = \"<b>head of the year</b>\" = New Year's, first day</li></ul>"},{"t":336.75,"lv":2,"h":"Genesis 1:1 - the Hebrew","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">OSB: \"In the beginning, God made heaven and earth\"</li><li class=\"d1\">seems simple - \"more to this\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><i>Bereshit bara Elohim hashamayim vehaaretz</i></li><li class=\"d1\"><i>bereshit</i> = \"in the beginning\" - back to it</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>bara</i> = <b>create</b> - back to it</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>Elohim</i> = <b>God</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><i>hashamayim</i> = <b>the heavens</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><i>mayim</i> inside it = <b>waters</b> → connected on <b>day two</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><i>haaretz</i> = the earth, the ground</li></ul>"},{"t":416.91,"lv":2,"h":"\"In the beginning\" as a summary statement","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">We hear \"in the beginning\" as <b>time</b> - first thing that happened</li><li class=\"d0\">But neither <i>bereshit</i> nor the Greek <i>en archē</i> says that</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>archē</i> = literally <b>first or primary</b> - direct translation of <b>head</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like an <b>archon</b> - occupies the <b>first place</b></li><li class=\"d0\">→ \"<b>in the first place</b>\" or \"<b>to begin</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">v.1 = <b>summary statement</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not: God created, <b>then</b> the rest happened after</li><li class=\"d1\">= what's <b>about to be narrated</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"To begin, God created the heavens and the earth\" - now the description</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>John's Gospel</b>: <i>en archē</i> \"in the beginning was the Word\"</li><li class=\"d1\">John uses Genesis all through, lining up with it</li><li class=\"d1\">first <b>18 verses</b> = <b>prologue</b>: summary, then life of Christ</li></ul>"},{"t":576.01,"lv":3,"h":"\"Created\" vs. \"made\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Made\" probably better as <b>created</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Different words in both languages</li><li class=\"d1\">Hebrew: <i>bara</i> = create; <i>asa</i> = <b>do or make</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Greek: <i>ktizō</i> = create; <i>poieō</i> = do or make</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Create</b> = original English sense: something that <b>didn't exist</b> now comes to be</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Make</b> doesn't imply that</li><li class=\"d1\">might be doing something done a <b>thousand times</b>; \"your <b>853rd chair</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">out of other things, <b>raw materials</b></li></ul>"},{"t":660.73,"lv":2,"h":"Creation from nothing?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Here's where the <b>accusations of heresy</b> come in\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Church and scriptures teach: God created <b>everything from nothing</b> - <i>creatio ex nihilo</i></li><li class=\"d0\">But <b>Genesis 1</b> doesn't describe that in the way we think as moderns</li><li class=\"d1\">other places <b>later in the OT</b> do</li><li class=\"d1\">this place comes at it from a <b>different perspective</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Creation told <b>several times</b> in the OT, different ways</li><li class=\"d1\">all agree God created; <b>different points</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>four Gospels</b>: same Christ who really lived, different points and perspectives</li></ul>"},{"t":733.86,"lv":2,"h":"No world before; no succession myth","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Point here: <b>no world before this</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient peoples: time <b>cyclical</b></li><li class=\"d1\">endless worlds before this one; nearly all believed in <b>at least one before</b>, usually ended with a <b>flood</b></li><li class=\"d1\">here: <b>no heavens, no earth</b> - God creates them</li><li class=\"d0\">Says something about <b>God</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Outside Israel nearly universal: the <b>succession myth</b> (scholars' term)</li><li class=\"d1\">older gods ruled; <b>insurrection</b> - son defeats / kills / <b>maims his father</b>, takes over</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Greek</b>: <b>Uranos</b> (heavens) and <b>Gaia</b> (earth) → <b>Titans</b>; <b>Kronos maims Uranos</b>; <b>Zeus</b> leads siblings against Titans - two cycles</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Babylon</b>: several</li><li class=\"d0\">God addresses it directly: <b>Isaiah</b> - \"I am God, before me there was <b>no other</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">already embedded here: no world → <b>no gods before him</b>, nothing before him</li><li class=\"d0\">Though he becomes God of <b>Abraham, Isaac, Jacob</b>, God of Israel - not just a local God</li><li class=\"d1\">not a <b>storm god</b>, not the God of <b>war</b></li><li class=\"d1\">none exist until he creates them - he \"makes all of them\", they don't <b>pre-exist or coexist</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"A lot in that first verse\"</li></ul>"},{"t":967.93,"lv":2,"h":"1:2: <i>tohu wabohu</i>","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.2 OSB: \"the earth was <b>invisible and unfinished</b>, and darkness was over the <b>deep</b>. The Spirit of God was <b>hovering</b> over the face of the water\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew: <i>tohu wabohu</i> - \"fun to say\", rhymes</li><li class=\"d1\">KJV: \"<b>without form and void</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><i>tohu</i> = <b>formless, chaotic, disordered</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><i>bohu</i> = <b>void</b>, empty</li></ul>"},{"t":1033.5,"lv":2,"h":"Not \"nothing\": being vs. chaos","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">How this differs from how we think about nothing</li><li class=\"d0\">Imagine nothing → a black or white space - \"<b>that's not actually nothing</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">an empty space is <b>something</b>; \"we can't conceive nothing\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Ancient people didn't try to imagine <b>nothingness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">being not opposed to nothingness - \"apologies to <b>Jean-Paul Sartre</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">being opposed to <b>chaos</b></li><li class=\"d1\">to exist = to be <b>something in particular</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no \"something that just exists but isn't anything\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1123.18,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: is matter eternal?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: coming round to saying <b>matter is eternal</b>? A: <b>No</b></li><li class=\"d1\">our matter = smallest particles, \"physical stuff\" - even that is <b>energy</b> bundles</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Aristotle's prime matter</b> = chaos: <b>isn't anything</b>, but has <b>potential</b> to become anything</li><li class=\"d1\">not a <b>void</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: like matter and energy? <b>Not really</b> - both are <b>something</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"potentiality\" there, <b>unactualized</b>; \"so it isn't anything\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1243.04,"lv":3,"h":"The Spirit hovering: brooding","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Imagery in the verse: <b>water</b> = <b>chaos</b> in the OT</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Hover</b>: <b>St. Basil</b> points to the <b>Syriac</b> word (Aramaic dialect) - he didn't know Hebrew</li><li class=\"d1\">used of a <b>mother bird brooding over her eggs</b></li><li class=\"d1\">egg imagery = <b>potential</b>, \"possibility\", not manifest</li><li class=\"d0\">Rest of the chapter: \"the <b>eggs hatch</b>\" - put in order, established</li><li class=\"d0\">→ passage not aimed at <b>ex nihilo</b> as usually thought - other passages teach that directly</li><li class=\"d1\">aimed at God taking things from <b>potential</b> to <b>being</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: anticipation? A: that's the <b>brooding</b> - active, not just hovering</li><li class=\"d0\">Picked up at <b>Christ's baptism</b>: Spirit descends like a <b>dove</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christ in the water; hymns (a few weeks off) link <b>Christ and Adam</b>, redeeming Adam in the waters</li></ul>"},{"t":1401.2,"lv":2,"h":"Two problems: formless and empty","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><i>Tohu</i> and <i>bohu</i> = <b>two problems</b> God will solve</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Problem 1, <i>tohu</i></b>: formless, <b>chaos</b></li><li class=\"d1\">brought out of chaos into existence; to exist = <b>to be in an order</b></li><li class=\"d1\">thing itself in order, <b>and</b> order <b>between</b> things - \"system of correct relationships\"</li><li class=\"d1\">later in OT = <b>justice</b>, then <b>peace</b>: everything in the right place</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>injustice</b> = order gets broken</li><li class=\"d1\">God takes things from chaos, puts in order <b>internally and with each other</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Problem 2, <i>bohu</i></b>: empty - specifically of <b>life</b></li><li class=\"d1\">chaos and life opposed; <b>sea</b> = image of chaos and death - humans <b>drown</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Resolved as creation goes forward; the <b>Spirit</b> already = anticipation</li></ul>"},{"t":1535.4,"lv":2,"h":"1:3-5: light, day one","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.3: \"<b>Let there be light</b>\" - God saw it was <b>good</b>; divided light from darkness; light = <b>day</b>, darkness = <b>night</b>; \"evening and morning, <b>one day</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew: actually \"<b>day one</b>\", not \"first day\"</li><li class=\"d1\">God speaks, light comes to be, then he <b>separates</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Step one of ordering</b> - first problem</li><li class=\"d0\">Light is <b>not yet the sun</b> - sun made on day four</li><li class=\"d1\">light and darkness <b>separated out</b>; first part of ordering</li><li class=\"d1\">so light created and still somehow mixed with darkness</li></ul>"},{"t":1606.32,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: darkness not created","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">God <b>doesn't create darkness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">darkness already \"over the face of the <b>deep</b>\" (<b>abyss</b> in Greek)</li><li class=\"d1\">he creates the <b>light</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>John's prologue</b> plays with it: \"the light shone in the darkness, and the darkness <b>didn't</b>...\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1637.47,"lv":2,"h":"What is this light? The fathers","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Icon of the first day</b>: light with <b>angelic faces and wings</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Many fathers</b>: creation of the <b>angels</b> - \"some disagreement\"</li><li class=\"d1\">separation of light from darkness = <b>fallen and unfallen</b> angels</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Other fathers</b>: connect to <b>day four</b> (sun, moon) and <b>day two</b>, different reasons - later</li><li class=\"d0\">Not sun and moon → <b>heavenly light</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Either</li><li class=\"d1\">the <b>light of the glory of God</b> shining into creation, <b>driving out darkness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">or angelic beings who <b>reflect or refract</b> it</li><li class=\"d0\">Darkness not abolished - sort of the <b>absence of light</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1749.94,"lv":3,"h":"Q&amp;A: Revelation's light","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: same as light from the <b>new Jerusalem</b> in Revelation? <b>Yes</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Fathers: the <b>uncreated light of God</b></li><li class=\"d1\">if God's light is in view → <b>glory of God himself</b> now shining <b>into creation</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God <b>manifesting himself</b> into creation, beginning to drive out darkness on that reading</li></ul>"},{"t":1795.45,"lv":2,"h":"1:6-8: the firmament","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">v.6: \"Let there be a <b>firmament</b> in the midst of the water, and let it <b>divide water from water</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">God made the firmament, divided water <b>under</b> from water <b>above</b></li><li class=\"d1\">called the firmament <b>heaven</b>; \"evening and morning, the <b>second day</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Greek word quite different from the Hebrew - OSB uses \"firmament\" because <b>KJV</b> established it</li><li class=\"d1\">never seen elsewhere - \"a <b>translation punt</b>\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1845.05,"lv":2,"h":"The <i>raqia</i>: dome and waters above","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Hebrew <i>raqia</i> = <b>barrier or partition</b></li><li class=\"d1\">image: a <b>dome</b> - water above, water below</li><li class=\"d1\">= creating the <b>sky</b>; the sea separated from it</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: sky is the separation → \"that's why it's <b>blue</b>\"?</li><li class=\"d1\">storms from up there, and storms from the sea</li><li class=\"d0\">Their cosmology: <b>God's throne, angels</b> above all that</li><li class=\"d1\">source of the <b>third heaven</b> idea later</li><li class=\"d1\">Second Temple Judaism: <b>three, seven, nine or ten</b> heavens - all <b>above</b> this</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Flood</b> (early next year): the <i>raqia</i> <b>opens up</b>, water dumps down - part of the cause</li></ul>"},{"t":1975.07,"lv":2,"h":"How literally to take this","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Putting things in order → <b>spaces</b>: day and night, sky and sea - but <b>still empty</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"As much as I hate going over this\": <b>how literally</b>?</li><li class=\"d0\">Not expected to believe</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>flat earth</b>, a literal <b>dome</b>, \"<b>satellites are a trick</b>\", \"man never went to the <b>moon</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">God not downloading <b>future physics</b> into Moses' brain</li><li class=\"d0\">Tradition: it's the <b>vision Moses received on Sinai</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like <b>St. John's vision</b> in Revelation - mistake: \"giant locusts spitting fire = <b>Apache helicopters</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">mistake to literalize into \"<b>biblical flat earther</b>\", earth on <b>pillars</b></li><li class=\"d0\">A vision to communicate <b>theological truths</b> and truths about the world</li></ul>"},{"t":2113.93,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: how literal are the days?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: \"one day equals...\" - how literally to take <b>time</b></li><li class=\"d0\">A vision → not so literal that Moses sat <b>24 hours</b> watching day one - \"pushing it too far\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Day four</b>: sun, moon, stars for <b>times and seasons</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>a day is a measure of time</b> - but three days passed before we could measure time?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"you can tie yourself up in a knot\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"don't know how far to go down this rabbit hole\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2216.62,"lv":3,"h":"Jewish calendar and Matthew Henry","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: <b>Jewish calendar</b> begins on day one - \"five thousand something\"? <b>Rabbinic</b>, yes</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Matthew Henry</b> (\"our Puritan friend\") did the same</li><li class=\"d1\">commentary on Genesis gives dates: \"<b>April 24th, 4062 BC</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">\"nailed right down\" - \"an <b>overreach</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">No humans there to <b>experience time</b> - long <b>before Moses</b>, though he sees it</li></ul>"},{"t":2279.62,"lv":2,"h":"Q&amp;A: what is time?","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: what do we mean? Is time a consequence of the <b>fall</b>? <b>No</b></li><li class=\"d0\">But time as we mean it = product of <b>human consciousness</b></li><li class=\"d1\">God <b>doesn't experience time as we do</b> - repeated in the Bible: \"a <b>thousand years</b> are like a day\" - not literal</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>angels</b> (created day one) - no reason they experience it as we do</li><li class=\"d0\">If time = <b>succession of events</b>: yes, things happen then other things</li><li class=\"d1\">what we usually call time = our <b>experience</b> of it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Deep time</b> = hypothetical time with <b>no observers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like \"deep space\": there, but we can't say anything about it</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: like the <b>possibility</b> - not yet formed → sun, moon, put in order</li><li class=\"d1\">most often time = an <b>order</b>; no order yet, can you talk about time?</li><li class=\"d1\">\"exploded people's brains\" - won't go further</li><li class=\"d0\">Point: don't force into a <b>\"super mega literal\"</b> mode</li></ul>"},{"t":2473.07,"lv":2,"h":"Aside: <i>Green Pastures</i>; time and the fall","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Aside: 1930s movie <i>Green Pastures</i> - angel: \"Lord, the batteries support the <b>firmament</b>\"; \"that'd be a whole lot of firmament\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Time doesn't come from the fall - but <b>open question in the fathers</b>: experienced <b>differently before the expulsion</b>?</li><li class=\"d1\">bears on the <b>world to come</b>: will we experience time differently?</li><li class=\"d1\">we assume \"then\" like \"now\" - succession of moments, <b>forever young</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Aside: younger = time feels faster? Theory: a year is <b>20% of a life at 5</b>, <b>2% at 50</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Important: time is <b>not an absolute thing</b> out there - <b>part of our experience</b></li><li class=\"d1\">like atoms: we don't experience them</li><li class=\"d1\">space travel, speed - older or younger</li></ul>"},{"t":2626.23,"lv":3,"h":"Dogs, saints' visions, angels","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Dogs</b> experience time differently</li><li class=\"d1\">gone 5 minutes or 5 hours → same reaction: \"they're gone, I'm sad; they're back, I'm happy\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Comment: saints' <b>visions</b> of saints - some young, some elderly - maybe our <b>overlay</b> of time on whatever it is</li><li class=\"d1\">\"filtering our perceptions through our understanding\"</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Genesis 3</b> (later): many fathers have <b>no big time lapse</b> between creation of humanity and <b>expulsion</b> - \"almost immediate\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Same for fathers who say <b>angels fell on day one</b></li><li class=\"d1\">angels wouldn't experience time as we do → <b>immediate</b> from their perspective</li></ul>"}]});
